@{
    ViewBag.Title = "CSS/LESS Rules";
}
<div class="col-sm-12">
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Must use LESS for your styling code</h2>
        <p>It adds features which allow variables, mixins, functions and many other techniques that make CSS be more maintainable, themeable and extendible.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Use valid CSS where possible</h2>
        <p>
            Unless dealing with CSS validator bugs or requiring proprietary syntax, you must ensure that your styling code is in valid form.
            Use tools such as the <a href="http://jigsaw.w3.org/css-validator/">W3C CSS validator</a> to test.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Use meaningful or generic ID and class names</h2>
        <p>
            Names that are specific and reflect the purpose of the element should be preferred as these are most understandable and the least likely to change.
            Using functional or generic names reduces the probability of unnecessary document or template changes.
        </p>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-danger">
/* Not recommended: meaningless */
#abc-123 {}</pre>
            <pre class="text-success">
/* Recommended: specific */
#gallery {}
#login {}
.video {}</pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Use ID and class names that are as short as possible but as long as necessary</h2>
        <p>
            Try to convey what an ID or class is about while being as brief as possible.
            It contributes to acceptable levels of understandability and code efficiency.
        </p>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-danger">
/* Not recommended */
#navigation {}
.atr {}</pre>
            <pre class="text-success">
/* Recommended */
#nav {}
.author {}</pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Avoid qualifying ID and class name with type selectors</h2>
        <p>
            It dues to performance reason.
        </p>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-danger">
/* Not recommended */
ul#example {}
div.error {}</pre>
            <pre class="text-success">
/* Recommended */
#example {}
.error {}</pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Prefer using class names as selectors to IDs</h2>
        <p>Using class name helps the CSS code is more independent from the JavaScript code.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Avoid descendant selector as possible</h2>
        <p>
            The descendant selectors are the most expensive selector in CSS.
        </p>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-danger">
/* Not recommended */
html body ul li a {
color: #fff;
}</pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Use shorthand properties where possible</h2>
        <p>
            The shorthand properties are useful for code efficiency and understandability.
        </p>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-danger">
/* Not recommended */
border-top-style: none;
font-family: palatino, georgia, serif;
font-size: 100%;
line-height: 1.6;
padding-bottom: 2em;
padding-left: 1em;
padding-right: 1em;
padding-top: 0;</pre>
            <pre class="text-success">
/* Recommended */
border-top: 0;
font: 100%/1.6 palatino, georgia, serif;
padding: 0 1em 2em;</pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Omit unit specification after “0” values</h2>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-success">
/* Recommended */
margin: 0;
padding: 0;</pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Omit leading “0”s in values between -1 and 1</h2>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-success">
/* Recommended */
font-size: .8em;</pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">For color values, use 3 character hexadecimal notation where possible</h2>
        <p>
            It make the code be shorter and more succinct.
        </p>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-danger">
/* Not recommended */
color: #eebbcc;</pre>
            <pre class="text-success">
/* Recommended */
color: #ebc;</pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Separate words in ID and class names by a hyphen</h2>
        <p>
            Do not concatenate words and abbreviations in selectors by any characters (including none at all) other than hyphens,
            in order to improve understanding and scannability.
        </p>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-danger">
/* Not recommended: does not separate the words “demo” and “image” */
.demoimage {}

/* Not recommended: uses underscore instead of hyphen */
.error_status {}
</pre>
            <pre class="text-success">
/* Recommended */
#video-id {}
.ads-sample {}</pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Put declarations in alphabetical order</h2>
        <p>
            The purpose of this action is to achieve consistent code in a way that is easy to remember and maintain.
            Ignore vendor-specific prefixes for sorting purposes.
        </p>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-success">
/* Recommended */
border: 1px solid;
-moz-border-radius: 4px;
-webkit-border-radius: 4px;
border-radius: 4px;
color: black;
text-align: center;
text-indent: 2em;</pre>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>